## Configuration

Settings come from environment variables such as `{{ package_name | upper }}_LOGGING__FORMAT=json`, where the upper-cased package name prefixes every variable and `__` separates a group from its field. A dotenv file is read only when you select it:

```bash
cp .env.example .env   # local overrides, gitignored
{%- if project_type == "cli" %}
uv run {{ project_slug }} --env-file .env settings
{%- else %}
uv run {{ project_slug }} serve --env-file .env
{%- endif %}
```

In Python, pass the same path to `load_settings(env_file=...)`.

Highest priority first: constructor arguments, environment variables, the `.env` file, defaults. A missing selected path, or a misspelled key inside a group, fails at startup.

To add a setting, add a field to a group in `src/{{ package_name }}/settings.py` (use `SecretStr` for secrets) and list it in `.env.example`. A test checks that they match. To read a TOML file as well, override `settings_customise_sources` on `Settings`, as the [pydantic-settings documentation](https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/#customise-settings-sources) shows.

## Logging

Logs go to stderr as text, or as JSON with `{{ package_name | upper }}_LOGGING__FORMAT=json`{% if project_type == "api" %}, uvicorn's access and error logs included{% endif %}. `{{ package_name | upper }}_LOGGING__LEVEL` sets the lowest level that is logged, in any letter case. Python warnings are logged too.
{%- elif project_type == "library" %}

## Logging

Use `logging.getLogger(__name__)` and leave handlers and levels to the application that imports this package.
{%- endif %}

## Development

```bash
uv run prek run --all-files --hook-stage manual   # every check{% if ci != "none" %}, as CI runs it{% endif %}
uv run --group docs zensical serve                # docs at http://localhost:{{ '8001' if project_type == 'api' else '8000' }}
```

Commits run lint, formatting and types, and check that the message is a [Conventional Commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/); pushes run the tests{% if web %}, the production build{% endif %} and the docs build.
{%- if ci == "gitlab" %}

For the weekly dependency audit, add a pipeline schedule in GitLab with `SCHEDULED_AUDIT` set to `1`. The `pages` job publishes the docs when run manually.
